def add(id,country,comp,y,n,r,topic,tags,src,s,sol,reason,notes=''):
 new.append(dict(id=id,country=country,competition=comp,year=y,number=str(n),statement=s,solution=sol,sourceUrl=src,solutionUrl=src,solutionKind='original-exposition',topic=topic,tags=tags.split('|'),difficulty=r,ratingReason=reason,reviewStatus='individually-assessed',notes='Statement restated; organizer paper and solutions reviewed. '+notes))
py='https://www.omapa.org/wp-content/uploads/2015/01/ex-omapa-2012-juv-r5-soluciones.pdf';py10='https://www.omapa.org/wp-content/uploads/2015/01/ex-omapa-2010-juv-rf-n3-soluciones.pdf';ec='https://sedem.org.ec/archivos/pruebas/PruebasOlimpiadas2024.pdf'
add('py-omapa-final-n3-2012-2','Paraguay','OMAPA Final Level 3',2012,2,5.5,'Combinatorics','Hamiltonian paths|Chessboard colouring|Induction',py,r'An ant moves between cells sharing a side, never visiting a cell twice. The top-left cell of an $n\times n$ checkerboard is black. From which starting cells can it visit every cell? Answer first for $n=4$ and $n=5$, then for every positive integer $n$.',r'''For even $n$, every starting cell works. For odd $n$, exactly the black cells work. In particular, all 16 cells work for $n=4$, and the 13 black cells work for $n=5$.

Necessity for odd $n$ follows from alternating colours: there is one more black cell than white cell, so a path visiting all cells must begin and end on black.

For even $n$, the grid has a cycle visiting every cell: reserve the first column, snake horizontally through each row of columns $2,\ldots,n$, and return along the first column. Breaking the cycle at any chosen starting cell gives the desired path.

For odd $n$, we prove sufficiency by induction. The $1\times1$ case is immediate. For $3\times3$, a corner start admits the usual row snake; from the centre, go to the top-middle cell and then around the outer ring to the middle-left cell. Rotations cover all black starts.

For odd $n\ge5$, remove the outer ring. If the starting cell is on that ring, choose an adjacent ring cell $w$ which is not a corner. Traverse the ring from the start to $w$ using its long route, then enter the inner grid through the cell next to $w$. That inner cell is black, so induction completes the path.

If the start is in the inner grid, take its inductive path. Some edge $uv$ of this path lies along the inner grid's boundary: any corner which is not an endpoint forces such an edge. The outer ring has adjacent cells $u',v'$ immediately outside $u,v$. Replace edge $uv$ by $u u'$, the long route around the outer ring from $u'$ to $v'$, and $v'v$. This inserts every ring cell exactly once and completes the induction.''','The colour obstruction is elementary, but sufficiency for every odd board and every allowable starting cell requires a genuine construction. The outer-ring insertion induction is the decisive additional argument.','The published solution gives the correct classification but does not prove general sufficiency from colour alternation alone; an explicit construction is supplied here.')
add('py-omapa-final-n3-2012-5','Paraguay','OMAPA Final Level 3',2012,5,4.5,'Geometry','Cyclic quadrilaterals|Similarity|Reciprocal lengths',py,r'Let $ABC$ be equilateral, let $Q$ lie in the interior of $BC$, and extend $AQ$ to meet the circumcircle again at $P$. Prove that $\frac1{PB}+\frac1{PC}=\frac1{PQ}$.',r'''Put $AB=BC=CA=s$. The angles subtending equal chords show that triangles $ABQ$ and $PCQ$ are similar, with $AB\leftrightarrow PC$ and $BQ\leftrightarrow PQ$. Thus
$$\frac1{PC}=\frac{BQ}{s\,PQ}.$$
Likewise, triangles $ACQ$ and $PBQ$ are similar, giving
$$\frac1{PB}=\frac{CQ}{s\,PQ}.$$
Adding and using $BQ+CQ=BC=s$ proves the identity.''','The reciprocal identity suggests splitting the base BC. Two complementary similarities obtained from the circumcircle turn the two reciprocals into those base pieces; finding the correct correspondence is the main step.')
add('py-omapa-final-n3-2010-5','Paraguay','OMAPA Final Level 3',2010,5,4,'Geometry','Orthocentre|Reflection|Homothety',py10,r"In a triangle $ABC$, let $D,E,F$ be the feet of the altitudes from $A,B,C$. Extend those altitude lines to meet the circumcircle again at $D',E',F'$. Assuming the indicated triangles are nondegenerate, prove that $D'E'F'$ is similar to $DEF$.",r'''Let $H$ be the orthocentre. The reflection of $H$ across $BC$ lies on the circumcircle: using directed angles modulo $180^\circ$, reflection changes $\angle BHC=180^\circ-\angle BAC$ into an angle subtending the same chord $BC$ as $A$. This reflected point lies on altitude $AD$, so it is $D'$. Consequently $D$ is the midpoint of $HD'$. Similarly, $E,F$ are the midpoints of $HE',HF'$.

The homothety centred at $H$ with ratio 2 therefore maps $D,E,F$ to $D',E',F'$, proving similarity.''','The key fact is that reflecting the orthocentre across a side places it on the circumcircle. Once this is established by cyclic angles, the three midpoint relations expose a single homothety.','The source statement tacitly uses a nondegenerate orthic triangle; this is stated explicitly.')
add('ec-sedem-juvenile-iii-2024-4','Ecuador','SEdeM Juvenile Level III',2024,4,4,'Number theory','Divisor counts|Prime factorisation|Digit sums',ec,r'Find every even positive integer $n<8000$ satisfying all of the following: $n$ has exactly 16 positive divisors; no exponent in its prime factorisation exceeds 3; the sum of its distinct prime divisors is 36; and $n$ and its decimal digit sum have the same remainder on division by 7.',r'''With prime exponents at most 3, a number with 16 divisors has one of the shapes $p^3q^3$, $p^3qr$, or $pqrs$, with distinct primes in each expression. As $n$ is even, one prime is 2. The first shape would require $2+q=36$, impossible. The last would require three odd primes summing to 34, also impossible by parity.

Thus the distinct primes are $2,q,r$, where $q+r=34$. Their possible unordered pairs are $(3,31),(5,29),(11,23)$. One of the three prime factors is cubed. Imposing $n<8000$ leaves
$$744,\quad1160,\quad2024,\quad1674,\quad7250.$$
Their remainders modulo 7 are respectively $2,5,1,1,5$, while their digit sums have remainders $1,1,1,4,0$. Only $\boxed{2024}$ satisfies the final condition.''','The divisor-count condition must first be converted into all possible exponent patterns. Parity removes two patterns, and the prime-sum condition reduces the remaining search to a short complete list. Several simple constraints must be combined in the correct order.')
add('ec-sedem-juvenile-iii-2024-5','Ecuador','SEdeM Juvenile Level III',2024,5,4.5,'Geometry','Orthic triangle|Inscribed angles|Trigonometry',ec,r'A circle has radius $\sqrt6$ and diameter $AB$. Choose $C,D$ on opposite sides of $AB$ such that $BC$ is the side length of an inscribed square and $BD$ is the side length of an inscribed equilateral triangle. Let $E,F,G$ be the feet of the altitudes of triangle $BCD$ from $B,C,D$, respectively. Find the perimeter of $EFG$.',r'''The chord lengths give $BC=2\sqrt3$ and $BD=3\sqrt2$. Since $AB$ is a diameter, $\angle ABC=45^\circ$ and $\angle ABD=30^\circ$. Thus $\angle CBD=75^\circ$, while the remaining angles at $C,D$ are $60^\circ,45^\circ$. By the sine rule,
$$CD=2\sqrt6\sin75^\circ=3+\sqrt3.$$
For an acute triangle, a side of the orthic triangle joining the two feet on sides through a vertex has length equal to the opposite side times the cosine of that vertex's angle; this follows directly by similarity of the right triangles. Therefore the required perimeter is
$$CD\cos75^\circ+BD\cos60^\circ+BC\cos45^\circ.$$
The three terms are $\sqrt6/2$, $3\sqrt2/2$, and $\sqrt6$, respectively. The answer is
$$\boxed{\frac32(\sqrt6+\sqrt2)}.$$''','One first reconstructs a 45–60–75 triangle from the chord data. The useful further insight is the relation between the sides of an acute triangle and its orthic triangle, avoiding cumbersome coordinates or Heron expansions.')
